5. Marketing Insights and Recommendations
Target the 17:00 period: Both selected weekdays peaked at 17:00. Prioritize campaign visibility, customer engagement and vehicle availability around the late-afternoon demand window.
Prioritize warmer periods: Hot conditions recorded the highest average demand at 4.97. Consider weather-aware campaigns during warmer periods, while treating this as an observed association rather than a causal effect.
Focus on July and Fall: July and Fall recorded the strongest monthly and seasonal averages respectively. These periods can inform campaign scheduling and resource planning.
Use clear-weather opportunities: Clear or partly cloudy conditions accounted for 67.28% of observations. Campaign planning should recognize this as the dominant operating environment.
Review overnight resource allocation: Demand was substantially lower during the overnight hours on the selected weekdays, particularly around 03:00-05:00. Promotional and operational resources can be reviewed for these low-demand periods.
Use weather forecasts as a planning input: The observed differences across temperature and weather categories support considering weather conditions alongside calendar timing when planning campaigns.
6. Conclusion
The 2017 analysis indicates that car-sharing demand was strongest around the late-afternoon period, particularly at 17:00, and was higher during warmer conditions. Saturday was the strongest weekday in the reported comparison, July was the strongest month, and Fall was the strongest season. Clear or partly cloudy weather was the most common condition, while Hot conditions recorded the highest average demand. These findings can help the marketing team schedule campaigns around stronger demand periods, consider weather conditions in campaign planning, and review resource allocation during low-demand overnight hours.


